        <!-- FAQ -->
        <section id="faq" class="ed-faq scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>Frequently asked questions</h2>
          <details open>
            <summary><h3>Does Vision Fitness still make treadmills?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              Yes, but only one on its US site on 5 November 2026: the light-commercial T600, with an LED console or as the T600E with a 22-inch touchscreen, sold through dealers. The home models such as the TF20, T40, T80 and T9200 are no longer listed; the recall notice says the recalled home models were sold until May 2025.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary><h3>Who owns Vision Fitness?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              Johnson Health Tech, the Taiwanese group that also owns Horizon, Matrix, Bowflex and Schwinn Fitness. Johnson Health Tech North America is the firm named on the 2026 Vision treadmill recall.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary><h3>How much is a Vision T600 treadmill?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              Vision publishes no price. On 5 November 2026 Fitness Supply, a US dealer, listed the touchscreen T600E at $8,799. Earlier US listings put the LED T600 at about $5,400, which we could not confirm on the day. Prices vary by dealer and region.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary><h3>What are the Vision T600 specs?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              Vision and a US dealer agree on a 4.2 HP AC drive, a 22 by 60-inch running surface, 0.5 to 12 mph on 120 V, 0 to 15% incline, a 400 lb user limit, 85 by 36 by 65 inches and 408 lb. It does not fold.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary><h3>Is the Vision T9200 a good treadmill?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              It was a basic home treadmill from the 2000s, with figures that vary by version: about 2.5 HP, 12 mph, 12% incline and 325 lb for the basic model in one review, and 3.0 CHP for the Deluxe. Any T9200 is now around 20 years old; buy one only for very little and expect to source parts from third parties.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary><h3>Which Vision treadmills were recalled?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              In January 2026 Johnson Health Tech recalled about 47,000 Matrix Retail and Vision treadmills, including the Vision T9800, TF20, T40 and T80 made from 2007 to January 2024, because the power cord can work loose and overheat. The remedy is a free locking power cord. A 2006 recall covered about 480 Premier consoles.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary><h3>What replaced Vision home treadmills?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              Johnson Health Tech’s other home brands. The Horizon 7.8 AT, $1,999 on 5 November 2026, has a 22 by 60-inch deck, 15% incline and 375 lb rating; Matrix covers the premium dealer market Vision’s top home models sold into.
            </p>
          </details>
        </section>

        <!-- Why trust us -->
        <section id="why-trust-us" class="ed-trust not-prose">
          <h2>Why trust Treadmill Reviews USA?</h2>
          <p>
            Every recommendation is checked against the numbers that matter for a treadmill: the maker's own
            listing and manual, at least one independent source, the warranty terms and the CPSC recall
            database. Where sources disagree we say so rather than pick a number, we say plainly when a
            machine has not been tested hands-on, and we never accept payment for a placement. <a href="/editorial-standards/">Read our review process.</a>
          </p>
